#
# ## fz-kdt.119 re-pricing
#
# `budget.codegen.functions` is `fz.codegen.define_function.stop` from
# `fz2 --emit=stats run <this fixture>`. It moved 35 -> 49 at fz-kdt.119, and
# every one of the fourteen is a `Kernel.dbg/1` or `Kernel.fz_dbg_value/1`
# specialization that BECAME LIVE: the argument's type is a union of
# `{:unknown | any}` at each of three positions, the tuple test used to see
# only "a 3-tuple" and collapsed the eight clauses into one destination, and a
# per-position test tells them apart, so eight arms are minted where one was.
# That 2^arity product is a real cost for a callee that reads no position,
# and fz-kdt.140 owns it.
#
# The number was 29 before this landing and 35 was already the truth, so the
# header was stale by six before anything here touched it. The other nine
# `budget.*` keys on this fixture have no producer anywhere in compiler2 --
# they name a "planner" that does not exist -- and fz-kdt.139 owns removing
# them corpus-wide.
#
# # receive_list_cons_pattern
#
# fz-puj.44 (X3) — receive matcher implementing SwitchKind::ListCons.
#
# Three messages sent (a non-empty list, an empty list, an atom) and three
# receives drain them in source order. Each clause set mixes `[h | _]`, `[]`,
# and a wildcard default so the matrix specialises ListCons with three keys
# (Cons → projects head via SubjectRef::ListHead; EmptyList; Nil-as-default).
#
# Locks that interp/JIT/AOT all bind `h` to the list's first element, route
# `[]` to the EmptyList arm, and fall through atoms to the wildcard.
fn main() do
  send(self(), [10, 20, 30])
  send(self(), [])
  send(self(), :stop)

  a = receive do
    [h | _] -> h
    [] -> -1
    _ -> 0
  end

  b = receive do
    [h | _] -> h
    [] -> -1
    _ -> 0
  end

  c = receive do
    [h | _] -> h
    [] -> -1
    _ -> 0
  end

  dbg({a, b, c})
end
